inline_comments.body:
Partly right. The validation test covered only one KV head per destination (4 query / 2 KV heads at CP2). The GPU CP test already covered MHA with 4 and 2 KV heads per destination ((8,8) at CP2 and CP4) and replicated GQA ((8,2) at CP4). Nothing covered GQA with several KV heads per destination, and no test reached the KV-slicing rejection paths.

`_cp_kv_head_slices_for_destinations` now has two CPU table tests, written below as (query heads, KV heads, CP size). The first pins the exact slices for:
- one KV head per destination: (4,2,2);
- two KV heads per destination: (8,4,2) and (16,8,4);
- MHA: (8,8,4);
- KV heads replicated over destinations: (8,2,4) and (4,1,2).

The second pins the four rejections:
- (6,3,2): local flash GQA cannot express the Q/KV grouping;
- (12,3,2): the CP boundary cuts unequal portions from two KV groups;
- (6,2,3): the destinations get unequal KV-head widths;
- (6,1,4): the query heads are not divisible by the CP size.

The validation tables now pass the stand-in's TP and CP group sizes as the caller's sizes, so a table entry reaches the CP KV-head check. The accept table covers CP2 with 8 query heads and 1, 2 or 4 KV heads, and TP2 x CP2 with 8 query and 4 KV heads per TP rank. The reject table adds 6 query / 3 KV heads at CP2, so the validator is checked to reject a grouping the CP path cannot run. The GPU CP test adds (8,4): two KV heads per destination at CP2 and one at CP4.

The file is the CPU home for `shared_prefix_layout.py` in the `tests/unit_tests/shared_prefix/` directory from the last round. It should not hold just one test, so it stays and now has ten. The `__post_init__` rejection and normalization tests from the [other thread](https://github.com/NVIDIA/Megatron-LM/pull/8156#discussion_r4424746986) live here.

A two-root forest with per-branch padding now covers the forest-layout methods:
- root offsets, the attention forest and the dense branch lengths;
- dense rows that repeat their own root's prompt and never reach into the other root;
- padded position IDs with trailing topology padding;
- token multiplicities with and without `exclude_sequence_padding`, where the unmasked case equals the dense-row gather counts;
- MTP loss-group lengths with and without root counts;
- the `ValueError` for a physical length shorter than the forest or one of its stars.

`cp_local_indices` now returns the DSA zigzag positions (see the [`cp_local_indices` thread](https://github.com/NVIDIA/Megatron-LM/pull/8156#discussion_r4424662757)). Its tests pin the zigzag split at CP2. They also check that the ranks own every index exactly once at CP1, CP2 and CP4, and that invalid CP geometries are rejected.
